<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Right Triangle Area Calculator?</h2>
  <p>A right triangle area calculator finds the two-dimensional area enclosed by a right triangle's three sides. For example, a right triangle with legs <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ m}]]')} /> has area <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{3\\times4}{2}=6\\text{ m}^2]]')} />: the triangle occupies six square metres of surface. Area is measured in square units because it counts flat space, not length.</p>
  <p>The perpendicular legs simplify the calculation enormously. Because the two legs meet at 90 degrees, one leg can serve as the base and the other as the height, so the area is just half their product. That simplicity is one reason the legs formula is the first method most students learn.</p>
  <p>Several methods exist for the same area because real problems hand you different measurements. A carpenter measures the two legs of a panel; a surveyor measures a hypotenuse and an angle; a geometer working with an inscribed circle knows the inradius and semiperimeter. Each method turns its own set of known values into the same area. The calculator on this page offers five modes matching these situations: Area from Legs, Area Variation (Angle A), Area Variation (Angle B), Area from Semiperimeter, and Area Variation (Altitude). The best method is usually the one that uses the measurements you already have, without forcing extra conversions first.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Understanding the area -->
<section id="sec-triangle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Area of a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>This guide uses one consistent notation throughout: <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> are the two acute angles, <span set:html={rich('[[C=90^\\circ]]')} /> is the right angle, side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> lies opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> lies opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />, side <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> is the perpendicular altitude from vertex <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> to the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> is the inradius, <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> is the semiperimeter, and <span set:html={rich('[[K]]')} /> is the area.</p>

  <h3>What Is a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has one angle of exactly 90 degrees. Its two legs meet at the right angle, and the hypotenuse lies opposite that angle. The hypotenuse is always the longest side, and the other two angles are acute: because the three angles sum to 180 degrees, <span set:html={rich('[[A+B=90^\\circ]]')} />. The two legs are perpendicular, which is the property that makes the area formula so direct.</p>

  <h3>What Does Area Mean?</h3>
  <p>Area measures the two-dimensional space enclosed by the triangle's three sides. It differs from perimeter, which measures the total boundary length, from side length, which measures one dimension, and from volume, which measures three-dimensional space. A triangular piece of paper has area equal to the amount of paper it covers; a triangular garden section has area equal to the ground it occupies. Both are reported in square units such as square metres, because area counts unit squares that fit inside the shape.</p>

  <h3>Why Is the Area Divided by Two?</h3>
  <p>Two identical right triangles can be combined along their hypotenuses to form a rectangle. If each triangle has perpendicular legs <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> the rectangle has area <span set:html={rich('[[ab]]')} />. One triangle occupies exactly half of that rectangle, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ab}{2}}]]]')} />. The division by two is not a fudge factor: it is the geometric consequence of a triangle being half a rectangle, and it appears in every area method on this page in one form or another.</p>

  <h3>Why Can the Same Triangle Have Five Area Formulas?</h3>
  <p>The triangle's area does not change when a different measurement method is used. The methods simply start with different known values. Two legs give the simplest formula. A leg, the hypotenuse, and the included acute angle allow a sine-based formula. Inradius and semiperimeter provide an alternative geometric relationship. Hypotenuse and perpendicular altitude use the general base-height rule. All five methods must produce the same area when the inputs describe the same valid triangle, and that agreement is the strongest verification available: two methods, one triangle, one answer.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('area-anatomy.svg')} alt="Right triangle anatomy: legs a and b, hypotenuse c, right angle C, altitude h to the hypotenuse, formula K = ab/2" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Anatomy of a right triangle: C is the right angle, a and b are the legs, c is the hypotenuse, and h is the altitude to the hypotenuse.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: Method one, legs -->
<section id="sec-legs" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Area of a Right Triangle Using Its Two Legs</h2>
  <p>The legs formula is the starting point for right triangle area: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ab}{2}}]]]')} />. It needs only the two perpendicular sides, and everything else on this page can be seen as a variation that recovers this same quantity from different inputs.</p>

  <h3>When Should You Use the Legs Formula?</h3>
  <p>Use this method whenever both perpendicular sides are known. No angle, hypotenuse, inradius, or altitude calculation is required first. Direct measurement of the two legs is common in carpentry, tiling, and classroom problems, which is why the Area from Legs mode is the default choice for most everyday calculations.</p>

  <h3>How the Formula Works</h3>
  <p>The two legs form the base and perpendicular height of the triangle. The general triangle area formula is <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac{\\text{base}\\times\\text{height}}{2}]]')} />; since the legs are perpendicular, base and height can be taken directly as <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, giving <span set:html={rich('[[K=ab/2]]')} />. No trigonometry is involved, and no extra side needs to be found first.</p>

  <h3>Fully Worked Example: A 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ m}]]')} />. Step 1: write the formula <span set:html={rich('[[K=ab/2]]')} />. Step 2: substitute the values, <span set:html={rich('[[K=(3\\times4)/2]]')} />. Step 3: multiply, <span set:html={rich('[[K=12/2]]')} />. Step 4: divide by two, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The answer is six square metres, not six metres: the unit carries the meaning of the quantity.</p>

  <h3>Can You Swap the Two Legs?</h3>
  <p>Yes. Multiplication is commutative, so <span set:html={rich('[[ab=ba]]')} /> and therefore <span set:html={rich('[[ab/2=ba/2]]')} />. The area is unchanged if the two legs are entered in the opposite order, provided the calculator fields correspond to the correct leg lengths. This symmetry holds only for the legs: swapping a leg with the hypotenuse would break the formula.</p>

  <h3>What If the Legs Have Decimal Values?</h3>
  <p>Decimal measurements work in the same formula. With <span set:html={rich('[[a=3.5\\text{ cm}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=9.2\\text{ cm}]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(3.5\\times9.2)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=32.2/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=16.1\\text{ cm}^2}]]]')} />. The arithmetic is identical; only the precision of the inputs changes, so keep enough digits and round at the end.</p>

  <h3>What If One Leg Is Unknown?</h3>
  <p>A missing leg can sometimes be found first using the Pythagorean theorem or a known angle, and the legs formula applied afterward. Note that the legs mode itself needs both legs: it does not recover a missing side automatically. If only one leg and the hypotenuse are known, the missing-leg calculation is a separate step before this mode can be used.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Method two, angle A -->
<section id="sec-angle-a" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Find Right Triangle Area Using Side b, Hypotenuse c, and Angle A</h2>
  <p>This method is useful when side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, and acute angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> are known: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{bc\\sin(A)}{2}}]]]')} />. It is the calculator's Area Variation (Angle A) mode.</p>

  <h3>Why Does This Formula Use Sine?</h3>
  <p>The general area formula for two sides and their included angle is <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac12 xy\\sin(\\theta)]]')} />. Angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> lies between sides <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, so substituting <span set:html={rich('[[x=b]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[y=c]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\theta=A]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac12 bc\\sin(A)]]')} />. Sine accounts for the perpendicular component of one side: only the part of side <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> that stands perpendicular to <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> contributes height, and that perpendicular part is <span set:html={rich('[[c\\sin(A)]]')} /></p>

  <h3>Fully Worked Example: A 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ m}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(36.87^\\circ)\\approx0.6]]')} />, substitute: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(4\\times5\\times0.6)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=12/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. This agrees with the legs formula because 0.6 is exactly the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[a/c=3/5]]')} />: the sine form is the legs form in disguise, with <span set:html={rich('[[c\\sin(A)]]')} /> playing the role of the other leg.</p>

  <h3>What Happens If the Angle Changes?</h3>
  <p>The sine factor changes the area when the two side lengths and included angle are varied within a valid triangle. Do not imagine an angle changing while every side length stays fixed: in a real triangle the sides and angles are linked, so changing the angle reshapes the triangle and moves the area with it. The included angle is essential to this formula precisely because it encodes how open or closed the triangle is.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors in Angle A Mode</h3>
  <p>Five mistakes recur in this mode. Pairing angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> with the wrong side is the most frequent: this formula needs side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, not side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, because <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> are the sides enclosing angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. Entering the angle in the wrong unit breaks the sine value: this calculator expects degrees, so 36.87 degrees must not be entered as 0.6435 radians. Forgetting to divide by two doubles the answer. And entering a right angle or an otherwise non-acute angle in the acute-angle field describes no valid input for this mode; the angle must sit strictly between 0 and 90 degrees.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: Method three, angle B -->
<section id="sec-angle-b" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Find Right Triangle Area Using Side a, Hypotenuse c, and Angle B</h2>
  <p>This mode uses the other acute angle and its two adjoining sides: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ac\\sin(B)}{2}}]]]')} />. It is the calculator's Area Variation (Angle B) mode.</p>

  <h3>Why Does This Formula Work?</h3>
  <p>Sides <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> enclose angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />. Starting from the general formula <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac12 xy\\sin(\\theta)]]')} /> and substituting <span set:html={rich('[[x=a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[y=c]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[\\theta=B]]')} /> gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac12 ac\\sin(B)]]')} />. The structure is identical to the Angle A formula; only the labels move, because the geometry of the two acute angles mirrors itself.</p>

  <h3>Fully Worked Example: The Same 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ m}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[B\\approx53.13^\\circ]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(53.13^\\circ)\\approx0.8]]')} />, calculate: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(3\\times5\\times0.8)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=12/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The result matches the legs method and the Angle A method, as it must: all three describe the same triangle.</p>

  <h3>How Are Angles A and B Related?</h3>
  <p>The two acute angles are complementary: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{A+B=90^\\circ}]]]')} />. This gives the identities <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=\\cos(B)]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(B)=\\cos(A)]]')} />. In the 3-4-5 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)=0.6=\\cos(B)]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(B)=0.8=\\cos(A)]]')} />. These relationships explain why both angle-based modes calculate the same area when their inputs are consistent: one mode's sine is the other mode's cosine, and the triangle's geometry is unchanged whichever acute angle you start from.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors in Angle B Mode</h3>
  <p>The mirror-image mistakes appear here: using side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> instead of side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, pairing angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> with the wrong side, and confusing the two acute angles with each other. Forgetting the sine function or forgetting to divide by two are the same arithmetic slips as in Angle A mode. Finally, an angle that does not correspond to the measured triangle, for example entering 36.87 degrees where 53.13 degrees belongs, silently produces a wrong but plausible-looking area, so always check that the angle matches the side pair.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: Method four, inradius and semiperimeter -->
<section id="sec-inradius" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Find Right Triangle Area Using Inradius and Semiperimeter</h2>
  <p>This method needs only two numbers, the inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> and the semiperimeter <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=rs}]]]')} />. The inradius is the radius of the circle inscribed inside the triangle, the circle that touches all three sides. The semiperimeter is half the triangle's perimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=\\frac{a+b+c}{2}}]]]')} />. It is the calculator's Area from Semiperimeter mode.</p>

  <h3>Why Does Area Equal Inradius Times Semiperimeter?</h3>
  <p>Connect the incenter, the center of the inscribed circle, to all three vertices. This divides the triangle into three smaller triangles. Each smaller triangle has the same perpendicular height <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, because the inradius is the perpendicular distance from the incenter to each side. Adding their areas gives <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac12 ar+\\frac12 br+\\frac12 cr]]')} />. Factor out <span set:html={rich('[[r/2]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac12 r(a+b+c)]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[s=(a+b+c)/2]]')} />, it follows that <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=rs}]]]')} />. This relationship holds for any valid triangle, not just a right triangle, provided the correct inradius and semiperimeter are known.</p>

  <h3>Finding the Inradius of a Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>For a right triangle there is a convenient shortcut: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=\\frac{a+b-c}{2}}]]]')} />. Treat it as a right-triangle shortcut, not as the general inradius formula: for a general triangle the inradius comes from <span set:html={rich('[[r=K/s]]')} />, which is circular if you are trying to find the area. The shortcut works because the two tangent lengths from the right-angle vertex are equal and the algebra collapses neatly for the 90-degree case.</p>

  <h3>Fully Worked Example: A 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />. First the semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=(3+4+5)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{s=6}]]]')} />. Then the inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=(3+4-5)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{r=1}]]]')} />. The area is <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. The answer matches the legs formula, confirming that the incircle view and the legs view describe the same triangle.</p>

  <h3>Fully Worked Example: A 5-12-13 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13]]')} />. Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=(5+12+13)/2=15]]')} />. Inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=(5+12-13)/2=2]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=2\\times15]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=30\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. Verify with the legs formula: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(5\\times12)/2=30]]')} />. Both methods agree, which is the expected outcome whenever the side measurements are consistent.</p>

  <h3>Common Errors</h3>
  <p>Confusing the inradius with the circumradius is the signature mistake: the inradius touches the sides from inside, while the circumradius reaches the vertices from outside, and they are different numbers. Using the full perimeter instead of the semiperimeter doubles the answer. Applying the right-triangle inradius shortcut <span set:html={rich('[[r=(a+b-c)/2]]')} /> to a non-right triangle produces a meaningless value. Forgetting that the product <span set:html={rich('[[rs]]')} /> has square units, and feeding inconsistent side measurements into the <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> calculations, round out the common failures.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: Method five, hypotenuse and altitude -->
<section id="sec-altitude" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Find Right Triangle Area Using Hypotenuse and Altitude</h2>
  <p>This method uses the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> as the base and the perpendicular altitude <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> as the height: <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=\\frac{ch}{2}}]]]')} />. It is the calculator's Area Variation (Altitude) mode.</p>

  <h3>What Is the Altitude?</h3>
  <p>An altitude is a perpendicular line drawn from a vertex to the opposite side. In this mode the altitude begins at the right-angle vertex <span set:html={rich('[[C]]')} /> and meets the hypotenuse at 90 degrees. It is not the same as either leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> or <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />: the legs lie along the triangle's edges, while the altitude drops into the interior and lands on the hypotenuse. The right-angle marker where <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> meets <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the visual guarantee that the perpendicularity condition is met.</p>

  <h3>Why Does the Formula Work?</h3>
  <p>It is the general base-height formula with a particular choice of base. Start from <span set:html={rich('[[K=\\frac12\\times\\text{base}\\times\\text{perpendicular height}]]')} />. Choose the hypotenuse as the base, <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{base}=c]]')} />, and the perpendicular altitude as the height, <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{height}=h]]')} />. Therefore <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} />. Nothing about this derivation is specific to right triangles: any triangle's area can be written this way, but the right triangle's legs formula is simply faster when the legs are known.</p>

  <h3>Fully Worked Example: A 6-8-10 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\text{ m}]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[h=4.8\\text{ m}]]')} />. Calculate: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(10\\times4.8)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=48/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. This is the same area as a right triangle whose legs measure 6 m and 8 m, since <span set:html={rich('[[(6\\times8)/2=24]]')} />. Two different descriptions, one area.</p>

  <h3>How Can the Altitude Be Calculated?</h3>
  <p>When both legs and the hypotenuse are known, the altitude follows from equating the two area formulas: <span set:html={rich('[[ab/2=ch/2]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{h=\\frac{ab}{c}}]]]')} />. For the 6-8-10 triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[h=(6\\times8)/10=4.8\\text{ m}]]')} />. The altitude is shorter than the hypotenuse, as it must be: the perpendicular is the shortest path from the vertex to the line. Note that the altitude mode does not calculate a missing altitude on its own; the altitude must be known or derived first.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: Which method to choose -->
<section id="sec-choose" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Which Right Triangle Area Formula Should You Use?</h2>
  <p>Five methods, one area. The comparison below maps each set of known measurements to its formula and calculator mode. Pick the row that matches the numbers already in your hand.</p>
  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-[640px] border-collapse text-left text-[15px]">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/20">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-headline">Known measurements</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-headline">Recommended method</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-headline">Formula</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/10"><td class="py-3 pr-4">Both perpendicular legs <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /></td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Area from Legs</td><td class="py-3"><span set:html={rich('[[K=ab/2]]')} /></td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/10"><td class="py-3 pr-4">Leg <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /></td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Area Variation (Angle A)</td><td class="py-3"><span set:html={rich('[[K=bc\\sin(A)/2]]')} /></td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/10"><td class="py-3 pr-4">Leg <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /></td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Area Variation (Angle B)</td><td class="py-3"><span set:html={rich('[[K=ac\\sin(B)/2]]')} /></td></tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/10"><td class="py-3 pr-4">Inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, semiperimeter <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /></td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Area from Semiperimeter</td><td class="py-3"><span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} /></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="py-3 pr-4">Hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, perpendicular altitude <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /></td><td class="py-3 pr-4">Area Variation (Altitude)</td><td class="py-3"><span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Why the Legs Formula Is Often the Simplest</h3>
  <p>The legs formula needs only two measurements and no trigonometry. Multiply, halve, done. Whenever the two perpendicular sides are available, every other method is a longer route to the same number.</p>

  <h3>When the Sine Formula Is Useful</h3>
  <p>An angle-based method earns its place when the available measurements include an acute angle, one leg, and the hypotenuse, as they do in surveying sight lines or navigation triangles. If the angle were not known, you would first have to recover the missing leg before the legs formula could be used; the sine form skips that detour.</p>

  <h3>When the Semiperimeter Method Is Useful</h3>
  <p>The inradius-semiperimeter formula suits geometry problems that already involve an incircle, or situations where <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> are known from earlier steps. It is also an excellent independent cross-check: because it shares no arithmetic with the legs formula, agreement between the two strongly suggests both are right.</p>

  <h3>When the Altitude Formula Is Useful</h3>
  <p>The altitude method is the natural choice when the hypotenuse and its perpendicular height are the known quantities, which happens in decomposition problems where a triangle is split along its altitude. Like the legs formula, it is a plain base-height calculation with no trigonometry.</p>

