<!-- Section 9: How to use the calculator -->
<section id="sec-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Area Calculator</h2>
  <p>Follow these steps for any of the five modes. Choosing the correct mode matters more than anything else: the mode decides which formula runs, so a correct formula with the wrong mode still answers the wrong question.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the measurements already known.</strong> List which of the legs, hypotenuse, angles, inradius, semiperimeter, or altitude you actually have.</li>
    <li><strong>Select the calculator mode matching those measurements.</strong> Area from Legs, Area Variation (Angle A), Area Variation (Angle B), Area from Semiperimeter, or Area Variation (Altitude).</li>
    <li><strong>Read the labels of the required input fields.</strong> Each mode shows only the fields its formula needs.</li>
    <li><strong>Identify the legs, hypotenuse, angle, inradius, semiperimeter, or altitude as appropriate.</strong> Match each known measurement to its label, using the notation of this guide.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the first required measurement.</strong> For example, "Leg a" in the legs mode.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the remaining measurements.</strong> Keep the values in the fields their labels describe.</li>
    <li><strong>Select compatible units using the available unit selectors.</strong> All length inputs must share one unit system before they are multiplied.</li>
    <li><strong>For angle-based modes, enter the correct acute angle.</strong> This calculator expects angles in degrees. Enter angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> in the Angle A mode and angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> in the Angle B mode, never the right angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Check that all inputs are positive and describe a valid triangle.</strong> Lengths must be positive, and the hypotenuse must exceed either leg.</li>
    <li><strong>Click Calculate Area.</strong> The button runs the selected mode's formula.</li>
    <li><strong>Review the area result and solution steps.</strong> The calculator shows the formula, the substitution, and the final area in square units.</li>
    <li><strong>Use the live triangle visualization to verify the labels.</strong> Confirm the diagram's side and angle labels correspond to your intended measurements.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset when starting a new calculation.</strong> The Reset button clears all fields so the next calculation starts clean.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Complete Walkthrough: All Five Modes on a 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, with <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[B\\approx53.13^\\circ]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[h=2.4]]')} />, here is what each mode needs and returns. Every mode returns <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ square units}}]]]')} /></p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Area from Legs.</strong> Enter "Leg a" = 3 and "Leg b" = 4. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[K=ab/2]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(3\\times4)/2]]')} />. Result: 6 square units.</li>
    <li><strong>Area Variation (Angle A).</strong> Enter "Leg b" = 4, "Hypotenuse c" = 5, "Angle A" = 36.87. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[K=bc\\sin(A)/2]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(4\\times5\\times0.6)/2]]')} />. Result: 6 square units.</li>
    <li><strong>Area Variation (Angle B).</strong> Enter "Leg a" = 3, "Hypotenuse c" = 5, "Angle B" = 53.13. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[K=ac\\sin(B)/2]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(3\\times5\\times0.8)/2]]')} />. Result: 6 square units.</li>
    <li><strong>Area from Semiperimeter.</strong> Enter "Inradius r" = 1 and "Semiperimeter s" = 6. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6]]')} />. Result: 6 square units.</li>
    <li><strong>Area Variation (Altitude).</strong> Enter "Hypotenuse c" = 5 and "Altitude h" = 2.4. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(5\\times2.4)/2]]')} />. Result: 6 square units.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Each mode uses a different set of known values, and none of them requires the same input fields. The shared answer is the point: five descriptions, one triangle, one area.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: Practical applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Applications of Right Triangle Area Calculations</h2>
  <p>Each example names the situation, the known measurements, the calculator mode that fits, why that mode fits, the formula, the step-by-step substitution, the final answer in square units, a plain-English interpretation, a verification idea where useful, and the assumptions behind the triangle model.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: Calculating the Area of a Triangular Garden</h3>
  <p>A garden contains a right-triangular planting section with perpendicular legs of 6 m and 8 m. Choose Area from Legs, the natural fit when both legs are measured directly: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(6\\times8)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The section covers 24 square metres of planting surface. This assumes the garden section is accurately represented by a right triangle with straight edges.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: Measuring a Triangular Plywood Panel</h3>
  <p>A carpenter cuts a right-triangular panel with legs measuring 1.2 m and 0.8 m. Choose Area from Legs: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(1.2\\times0.8)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=0.96/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=0.48\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The panel covers just under half a square metre, which helps estimate material coverage. Cutting allowances and material waste are not included in this geometric area.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: Finding the Area of a Triangular Sail</h3>
  <p>A triangular sail section has perpendicular legs of 5 m and 12 m. Choose Area from Legs: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(5\\times12)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=30\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The formula gives the flat area of the triangular section, useful for cloth estimates before seams and curvature are considered.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: Estimating a Right-Triangular Land Section</h3>
  <p>A land survey identifies a right-triangular section with perpendicular sides of 30 m and 40 m. Choose Area from Legs: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(30\\times40)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=600\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. This is the area of the specified triangular section, not necessarily the total area of the surrounding property. Real land boundaries may require more detailed surveying than a single triangle model.</p>

  <h3>Example 5: Calculating a Roof Gable's Area Using Angle A</h3>
  <p>A simplified triangular roof section has <span set:html={rich('[[b=6\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=10\\text{ m}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx53.13^\\circ]]')} />. Choose Area Variation (Angle A), since a leg, the hypotenuse, and the included angle are the measured data. With <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(53.13^\\circ)\\approx0.8]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(6\\times10\\times0.8)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The result is the area of the triangular cross-section. A real roof's total surface area depends on its three-dimensional geometry and other roof sections.</p>

  <h3>Example 6: Finding the Area of a Triangular Awning</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular awning section has <span set:html={rich('[[b=4\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ m}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Choose Area Variation (Angle A): <span set:html={rich('[[K=bc\\sin(A)/2]]')} />, and with <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(36.87^\\circ)\\approx0.6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=(4\\times5\\times0.6)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. This assumes the measurements describe a flat right triangle, which holds for a tensioned rectangular awning cut on the diagonal.</p>

  <h3>Example 7: Calculating a Triangular Support Panel Using Angle B</h3>
  <p>A support panel has <span set:html={rich('[[a=3\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5\\text{ m}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[B\\approx53.13^\\circ]]')} />. Choose Area Variation (Angle B): <span set:html={rich('[[K=(3\\times5\\times0.8)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The angle-based formula finds the area without entering both legs directly, which helps when the second leg was never measured.</p>

  <h3>Example 8: Finding the Area of a Truss Section</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular truss section has <span set:html={rich('[[a=5\\text{ m}]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13\\text{ m}]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[B\\approx67.38^\\circ]]')} />. Choose Area Variation (Angle B). Since <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(67.38^\\circ)\\approx12/13]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(5\\times13\\times(12/13))/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=30\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. This is the geometric area of the triangular section, not a structural load-capacity calculation.</p>

  <h3>Example 9: Calculating Area From Inradius and Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>Consider a 3-4-5 right triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[r=1]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} />. Choose Area from Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=6\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. The inradius and semiperimeter give an alternative route that never multiplies the two legs, yet lands on the same answer.</p>

  <h3>Example 10: Finding the Area of a 5-12-13 Triangular Plot</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular plot has sides <span set:html={rich('[[a=5]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=12]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=13]]')} />. Choose Area from Semiperimeter. Semiperimeter: <span set:html={rich('[[s=(5+12+13)/2=15]]')} />. Inradius: <span set:html={rich('[[r=(5+12-13)/2=2]]')} />. Area: <span set:html={rich('[[K=2\\times15]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=30\\text{ square units}}]]]')} />. Verify with the legs formula: <span set:html={rich('[[K=(5\\times12)/2=30]]')} />. Both methods agree because both describe the same plot.</p>

  <h3>Example 11: Estimating a Triangular Panel's Area From Altitude</h3>
  <p>A triangular panel is represented by a right triangle whose hypotenuse measures 10 m and whose perpendicular altitude to the hypotenuse is 4.8 m. Choose Area Variation (Altitude): <span set:html={rich('[[K=ch/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[K=(10\\times4.8)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=24\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. The altitude must be perpendicular to the hypotenuse: any other line from the vertex to the hypotenuse is longer and would overstate the area.</p>

  <h3>Example 12: Calculating a Triangular Section From Hypotenuse and Altitude</h3>
  <p>A right-triangular section has a hypotenuse of 20 m and a perpendicular altitude of 9 m. Choose Area Variation (Altitude): <span set:html={rich('[[K=(20\\times9)/2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{K=90\\text{ m}^2}]]]')} />. This is the area of the triangle when the measurements describe the same valid right triangle, which they do here: 9 m is well under the hypotenuse length, as a perpendicular must be.</p>

