<!-- Section 11: Verification -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Verify the Area of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>One 3-4-5 triangle, five methods, one answer. Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c=5]]')} />, with <span set:html={rich('[[A\\approx36.87^\\circ]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[B\\approx53.13^\\circ]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[r=1]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[s=6]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[h=ab/c=12/5=2.4]]')} />:</p>
  <p><strong>Method 1, Legs:</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[K=(3\\times4)/2=6]]')} />. <strong>Method 2, Angle A:</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[K=(4\\times5\\times0.6)/2=6]]')} />. <strong>Method 3, Angle B:</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[K=(3\\times5\\times0.8)/2=6]]')} />. <strong>Method 4, Inradius and semiperimeter:</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[K=1\\times6=6]]')} />. <strong>Method 5, Hypotenuse and altitude:</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[K=(5\\times2.4)/2=6]]')} /></p>
  <p>These are five mathematical routes to the same geometric quantity. The triangle's area does not depend on which correct formula is used; it depends on the actual geometry of the triangle. Whenever two independent methods agree on your triangle, you can trust the number.</p>

