</section>

<!-- Section 12: Mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Right Triangle Area</h2>
  <p>Area errors are rarely arithmetic failures. They are labeling failures, formula mix-ups, and unit slips. The eighteen below cover nearly every wrong answer this page's calculator can produce.</p>

  <h3>1. Forgetting to Divide by Two</h3>
  <p>Multiplying the legs without halving gives the rectangle's area, not the triangle's. With legs 3 and 4, <span set:html={rich('[[3\\times4=12]]')} /> is wrong; <span set:html={rich('[[(3\\times4)/2=6]]')} /> is right. Fix: end every legs or base-height calculation with the division by two.</p>

  <h3>2. Using the Hypotenuse as One of the Perpendicular Legs</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is never a leg. Entering c = 5 as a leg with the other leg 3 gives <span set:html={rich('[[(5\\times3)/2=7.5]]')} /> instead of 6. Fix: identify the longest side first and reserve it for the hypotenuse field.</p>

  <h3>3. Pairing Angle A With the Wrong Side</h3>
  <p>The Angle A formula needs side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, the side enclosing angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> with the hypotenuse. Entering side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> instead computes the wrong product. Fix: check that angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> sits between sides <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> before entering values.</p>

  <h3>4. Pairing Angle B With the Wrong Side</h3>
  <p>The mirror image: the Angle B formula needs side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, not side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />. Fix: confirm that angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> sits between sides <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /></p>

  <h3>5. Using the Wrong Included Angle in a Sine Formula</h3>
  <p>Entering 36.87 degrees where 53.13 degrees belongs, or vice versa, produces a plausible but wrong area. Fix: match the angle to the side pair: Angle A goes with <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, Angle B with <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /></p>

  <h3>6. Confusing Altitude With a Leg</h3>
  <p>The altitude <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> drops from the right-angle vertex to the hypotenuse; a leg runs along the triangle's edge. Entering a leg as the altitude overstates the height. Fix: the altitude must land on the hypotenuse with a right-angle marker.</p>

  <h3>7. Using a Line That Is Not Perpendicular as the Altitude</h3>
  <p>Any non-perpendicular line from the vertex to the hypotenuse is longer than the true altitude, so the area comes out too large. Fix: verify perpendicularity, by construction or by the <span set:html={rich('[[h=ab/c]]')} /> check, before using the altitude mode.</p>

  <h3>8. Confusing Inradius With Circumradius</h3>
  <p>The inradius touches the sides from inside; the circumradius reaches the vertices from outside. In a right triangle the circumradius is <span set:html={rich('[[c/2]]')} />, always larger than the inradius. Entering the circumradius into the <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} /> formula inflates the area. Fix: use <span set:html={rich('[[r=(a+b-c)/2]]')} /> for the right-triangle inradius.</p>

  <h3>9. Using the Full Perimeter Instead of the Semiperimeter</h3>
  <p>The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[K=rs]]')} /> with <span set:html={rich('[[s]]')} /> the semiperimeter. Entering the full perimeter 12 instead of 6 in the 3-4-5 triangle gives 12 instead of 6. Fix: halve the perimeter before multiplying by <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /></p>

  <h3>10. Mixing Incompatible Measurement Units</h3>
  <p>Legs of 3 metres and 40 centimetres are not 3 and 40 in one formula. Fix: convert every input to one unit, for example 3 m and 0.4 m, before calculating.</p>

  <h3>11. Reporting Square Metres as Metres</h3>
  <p>An area of 6 m² is not 6 m. The square unit is part of the answer's meaning: it says how much surface, not how much length. Fix: always write the square unit with the number.</p>

  <h3>12. Rounding the Sine Value Too Early</h3>
  <p>Using 0.6 for sine when the true value is 0.6000 is harmless, but rounding 0.9231 to 0.9 mid-calculation shifts the result. Fix: keep full precision through every step and round only the final answer.</p>

  <h3>13. Entering a Right Angle or Invalid Angle Into an Acute-Angle Field</h3>
  <p>The angle modes expect an acute angle between 0 and 90 degrees. Entering 90 degrees or a negative value describes no valid input for these modes. Fix: enter the acute angle the triangle actually has, in degrees.</p>

  <h3>14. Assuming All Five Modes Require the Same Measurements</h3>
  <p>Each mode asks for a different set of inputs: legs mode needs two legs, the Angle A mode needs a leg, the hypotenuse, and an angle, and the semiperimeter mode needs an inradius and a semiperimeter. Fix: read the field labels of the mode you selected.</p>

  <h3>15. Assuming a Larger Perimeter Always Means a Proportionally Larger Area</h3>
  <p>Perimeter and area scale differently: doubling all sides quadruples the area. A triangle with a larger perimeter can even have a smaller area if it is long and thin. Fix: compare areas with area formulas, never with perimeters.</p>

  <h3>16. Forgetting That the Same Area Can Be Calculated in Multiple Ways</h3>
  <p>Treating each method as a separate fact misses the built-in verification: if two methods disagree, something is wrong with the inputs or the mode choice. Fix: run a second method and investigate any mismatch.</p>

  <h3>17. Entering Side Lengths That Cannot Describe the Intended Triangle</h3>
  <p>Sides that fail the Pythagorean check, such as 2, 3, and 10, describe no right triangle at all. Fix: verify <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} /> before trusting any area built from those sides.</p>

  <h3>18. Using the Right-Triangle Inradius Shortcut for a Non-Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>The formula <span set:html={rich('[[r=(a+b-c)/2]]')} /> is derived from the 90-degree geometry. Applying it to an acute or obtuse triangle produces a meaningless inradius. Fix: use this shortcut only when the triangle is confirmed right-angled.</p>

  <h3>Wrong Versus Correct: A Concrete Example</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a=3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b=4]]')} />, an incorrect calculation skips the halving: <span set:html={rich('[[K=3\\times4=12]]')} />. That is the area of the corresponding 3-by-4 rectangle, not the right triangle. The correct calculation is <span set:html={rich('[[K=(3\\times4)/2=6]]')} />. The triangle occupies half the rectangle, so the division by two is what turns a rectangle answer into a triangle answer.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: Validation -->
<section id="sec-validate" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Area Result Is Valid</h2>
  <p>Six checks catch the vast majority of invalid results before they leave the page.</p>

  <h3>Check 1: Positive Measurements</h3>
  <p>All required lengths must be positive: <span set:html={rich('[[a>0]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b>0]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[c>0]]')} />. For the inradius mode, <span set:html={rich('[[r>0]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[s>0]]')} />, and the altitude must be positive too. Zero or negative inputs describe no triangle.</p>

  <h3>Check 2: Valid Triangle Side Lengths</h3>
  <p>For a right triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} /> must hold, and the hypotenuse must be longer than either leg. If the sides fail this check, the inputs are inconsistent and the area is meaningless.</p>

  <h3>Check 3: Correct Angle-Side Pairing</h3>
  <p>Angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> pairs with sides <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> in the included-angle formula; angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> pairs with sides <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. Confirm the pairing before accepting an angle-based result.</p>

  <h3>Check 4: Correct Altitude</h3>
  <p>The altitude <span set:html={rich('[[h]]')} /> must be perpendicular to the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. A quick sanity test: the altitude is always shorter than the hypotenuse and shorter than either leg.</p>

  <h3>Check 5: Correct Area Units</h3>
  <p>If the input lengths are in metres, the area must be in square metres; centimetres give square centimetres. The unit of the answer is the square of the input unit, never the input unit itself.</p>

  <h3>Check 6: Cross-Check With Another Method</h3>
  <p>Use the 3-4-5 triangle to compare the legs formula with the semiperimeter formula: <span set:html={rich('[[(3\\times4)/2=6]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[1\\times6=6]]')} />. Both must give 6 square units. Small discrepancies in angle-based calculations may come from rounded angle measurements or rounded trigonometric values; larger ones signal a real error. This guide states no precision tolerance or accuracy guarantee for the calculator: the check is about consistency, not about promised precision.</p>
</section>

